𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟲/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗲𝗿 𝗠𝗲𝗺𝗼𝗿𝘆 𝗜𝘀 𝗙𝘂𝗹𝗹

🎯 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“Your production server is running out of memory. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I would not immediately restart the server.

First, I want to understand how memory is being consumed.

I would start with:

free -m

This gives me an overview of total, used, free, available memory and swap.

Then I would identify processes consuming the most memory:

ps -eo pid,ppid,cmd,%mem,%cpu --sort=-%mem | head

I can also use:

top

and press M to sort processes by memory usage.

Next, I would check whether the system is using swap:

swapon --show

I would also check:

vmstat 1

This helps me understand whether the system is under memory pressure and actively swapping.

Then I would investigate the application.

I would check:

• Is one process continuously increasing memory usage?
• Did memory usage suddenly increase?
• Was there a recent deployment?
• Is there a memory leak?
• Is traffic higher than normal?
• Are there errors in application logs?
• Is the server configured with enough memory for the workload?

If one application process is consuming excessive memory, I would not immediately kill it.

First, I would understand the impact and follow the approved incident/recovery procedure.

If restarting the affected service is the approved recovery action, I would restart that service rather than rebooting the entire server.

After recovery, I would monitor:

free -m
top
vmstat 1

and verify that the application is functioning normally.

Then I would perform Root Cause Analysis.

Issue → Investigation → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

For prevention, I might consider memory monitoring, alerting, application tuning, capacity planning, or fixing a memory leak.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

When memory is high, don't just look at the “free” value.

Understand:

𝗠𝗲𝗺𝗼𝗿𝘆 𝗨𝘀𝗮𝗴𝗲 → 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗰𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗲𝘀 → 𝗦𝘄𝗮𝗽 → 𝗠𝗲𝗺𝗼𝗿𝘆 𝗣𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘀𝘂𝗿𝗲 → 𝗥𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗖𝗮𝘂𝘀𝗲

That's the difference between knowing Linux commands and troubleshooting production systems. 🚀
